cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  Published as version 2.0.0, the first archived release of this technical note
  (DOI 10.5281/zenodo.24171237). Versions 1.0.x-1.2.0 were internal drafts and were
  never deposited. If you cite this note, please cite the version you read.
  The DOI below belongs to THIS NOTE, not to the repository as a whole: the
  repository's root CITATION.cff cites a separate paper.
title: "Certified Weil Positivity in Two Windows: Spectral Bounds, Conditioning, and Reproducible Certificates"
abstract: >-
  Computer-assisted two-sided bounds on the spectral infimum of the window Weil
  form in one fixed normalization, at two explicit windows:
  1.43300048827856e-17 <= lambda_Q(4/5) <= 1.674e-17 and
  8.27343868254787e-18 <= lambda_Q(L~) <= 9.475e-18, where L~ =
  80471895621705018/10^17 is a rational strictly below (log 5)/2. Each lower
  bound comes from a frequency-cutoff comparison at T = 400 reduced to a
  440-dimensional Legendre block, verified by a preconditioned congruence in Arb
  ball arithmetic with a super-exponentially small complement estimate and a
  strict Schur-type coupling test, in both parity sectors. Each upper bound is
  the Rayleigh quotient of the original form at one explicit even rational test
  function, evaluated in closed form with no quadrature. Monotonicity in L
  extends the first lower bound to 0 < L <= 4/5, the second to 0 < L <= L~, and
  the second upper bound to every L >= L~, including exactly (log 5)/2; no
  positive lower bound at (log 5)/2 follows. Pairing the four endpoints gives
  0.494 <= lambda_Q(L~)/lambda_Q(4/5) <= 0.662. At L = 4/5 the note certifies
  per-sector conditioned coercivity (four explicit even directions leave the
  infimum at least 0.6175) and that exactly four even eigenvalues lie below
  1e-2, with only "at most three" certified in the odd sector. A proved
  normalization dictionary identifies the form with the window form of
  arXiv:2611.13129v1 under lambda = exp(L). This is a computation at two
  windows; it establishes no arithmetic estimate, nothing about the location of
  any zeta zero, and nothing about the Riemann hypothesis. Technical note, not
  peer-reviewed.
